! mcp_bootstrap_p176.strap -- THE TAIL OF THE "F WIT" INTERRUPT-TABLE
! MASTER COPY: slots 24 through 47, twenty-four full words of
! "SIC,STIC; BD,SIPT+n.", printed page 176.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.183.  The header was READ, not
! assumed: "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID KE000083
! DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  176".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 220650. <<<
! Rendered at 600 dpi (crop x=6800 y=250 W=2600 H=400 of the 600-dpi
! page): the word "LOCATION" is printed at the right-hand end of the
! heading row and SIX DIGITS FOLLOW IT, "220650", clear of the dashed
! rule.  Nothing was invented off the rule and nothing was taken from
! the survey OCR.  That is LINE 1'S location word
! (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and line 1 here IS a
! located row, which is exactly the case the rule predicts to be
! numbered rather than blank.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FORTY-EIGHT PRINTED LINES AND NOTHING ELSE.  No comment card, no
! asterisk banner, no SLC, no DD, no SYN, no VF, no reservation, no
! NAME in the NAME column on any of the forty-eight rows.  The page
! is CONTIGUOUS from 220650.00 to 220677.40 -- twenty-four words,
! forty-eight half words, no hole of any kind -- and then blank
! paper to the foot of the form.
!
! The forty-eight lines are twenty-four copies of one shape:
!
!     SIC,STIC           215265.00 80      (even half word)
!     BD,SIPT+n.         2151nn.04 00      (odd  half word)
!
! with n running 24, 25, ... 47 without a gap.
!
! >>> C22-6728-1 NAMES THIS SHAPE IN TYPESET PROSE. <<<  "THE
! INTERRUPT TABLES ... All IBM 7030 interrupts are received by the
! interrupt table (SIT).  It consists of 48 full word instructions
! of the form SIC,---; BD,---; one for each indicator 0 through 47."
! That is this page, twenty-four of those forty-eight words, for
! indicators 24 through 47 -- the I-O and maskable indicators, which
! the same page of the guide says are "all handled by a common
! routine ... control is passed to these routines through the
! parallel interrupt table".  Every one of this page's BD operands
! is an SIPT slot, and every one of its SIC operands is STIC, the
! stored instruction counter.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THIS IS NOT THE SIT.  IT IS THE "F WIT" MASTER COPY OF IT,
! AND THE TWO ARE BIT-IDENTICAL WORD FOR WORD. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The live SIT is at 215023.00 (printed p.110's own label "S IT
! SIC,YMFIC").  This page is at 220650, five thousand words away,
! and it prints the SAME WORDS as SIT slots 24..47.  Checked
! mechanically against the two LANDED decks that carry those slots:
!
!   SIT slot 24..44  = 215053..215077  -- decks/mcp_bootstrap_p111
!   SIT slot 45..47  = 215100..215102  -- decks/mcp_bootstrap_p112
!
! All forty-eight half words agree, digit for digit, with the
! !CHECK lines those two decks already declare.  MEASURED, not
! inferred: the two decks' !CHECK locations were collected
! SEPARATELY and NO location appears in both, and the forty-eight
! SIT half words resolve 42 from decks/mcp_bootstrap_p111 (slots
! 24..44) and 6 from decks/mcp_bootstrap_p112 (slots 45..47), with
! zero missing and zero disagreeing.  So the per-row attribution in
! the !CHECK notes below is a measurement, not a reading of p.111's
! header prose.  Example:
!
!   p.111 !CHECK 215053.00 | 215265.00 80 | line  9 SIC,ST IC
!   p.111 !CHECK 215053.40 | 215133.04 00 | line 10 BD,SIPT+24.
!   p.176 line 1 220650.00   215265.00 80              SIC,STIC
!   p.176 line 2 220650.40   215133.04 00              BD,SIPT+24.
!
! >>> IT CORROBORATES, IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH. <<<  Same drum, same
! volume (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "BE SURE THE SECOND
! READING IS INDEPENDENT").  But it is NOT the oracle's forbidden
! case either: these are DIFFERENT CARDS at DIFFERENT LOCATIONS on
! DIFFERENT PAGES read by DIFFERENT transcribers, not an OCR of this
! page's own ink.  Forty-eight-for-forty-eight agreement on that
! basis is the strongest thing on this page.
!
! WHERE THE COPY'S BASE IS, AND WHAT READS IT.  >>> THIS WAS
! SETTLED BY RENDERING printed p.175 (PDF p.182) AT 300 dpi, NOT BY
! THE VOLUME OCR. <<<  p.111's deck had reached the same place
! through that OCR and flagged it as its weakest row, unrendered --
! the row whose statement column the OCR returns as
! "FFC((((( (#U#C#C#N BOYSIFAED".  One command replaced it.  What
! p.175 PRINTS, read off the image, is its last eight lines:
!
!   49*  220644.00   215265.00 80    F WIT   SIC,STIC      KE000079
!   50*  220644.40   215127.04 00            BD,SIPT+20.   KE000079
!   51*  220645.00   215265.00 80            SIC,STIC      KE000080
!   52*  220645.40   215471.04 00            BD,SIFAE      KE000080
!   53*  220646.00   215265.00 80            SIC,STIC      KE000081
!   54*  220646.40   215131.04 00            BD,SIPT+22.   KE000081
!   55*  220647.00   215265.00 80            SIC,STIC      KE000082
!   56*  220647.40   215132.04 00            BD,SIPT+23.   KE000082
!
! and its line 15:
!
!   15*  220616.00   220620.00 84  000000.23 10
!                    KF(BU,64),FWIT-20.($4) -IS WD AN MCP ST FXUP
!
! F WIT is a NAME in p.175's NAME column at 220644.00, and 0o220644
! minus 20 DECIMAL (0o24) is exactly the 220620.00 that line 15
! prints as its first operand.  So F WIT is SIT SLOT 20 (compare
! decks/mcp_bootstrap_p110 line 48, "215047.40  215127.04 00
! BD,SIPT+20."), F WIT-20 is the copy's virtual slot 0 at 220620,
! and $4 holds the indicator number.  THE COPY THEREFORE RUNS
! 220644..220677 -- twenty-eight words, slots 20 through 47 -- AND
! THIS PAGE IS ITS LAST TWENTY-FOUR.
!
! WHAT THE F ROUTINE DOES WITH IT, also off p.175's printed English:
!
!   line 14  L(BU),SIT($4)                     (SIT = 215023.00)
!   line 15  KF(BU,64),FWIT-20.($4) -IS WD AN MCP ST FXUP
!   line 26  F INS ST(BU),SIT($4) -INSERT FIXUP INTO INTERPT TABLE
!   line 31  F STZ TI,1,FWIT-20.($4),SIT($4) -STORE ST MCP FXUP IN TABLE
!
! It compares the live interrupt-table word for indicator $4 against
! this master copy, and transmits the master copy back into the live
! table when it must restore MCP's standard fixup.  THIS PAGE IS THE
! THING BEING COMPARED AGAINST AND COPIED FROM.
!
! That reading is off a NEIGHBOURING page and is labelled as such;
! nothing in this deck's checks depends on it.  What this page owns
! on its own ink, with no reference to F WIT at all, is the
! twenty-four words at 220650..220677 and their bit-identity with
! SIT slots 24..47.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! COUNTED OFF THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN, AND IT IS TWENTY-FOUR CARDS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The serial column runs KE000083 (lines 1 AND 2) to KE000106 (lines
! 47 AND 48) -- TWENTY-FOUR serials for FORTY-EIGHT lines, each
! serial printed on exactly two consecutive lines.  Rendered at 400
! dpi beside the LINE column and read digit by digit; 106-83+1 = 24
! and 24*2 = 48, so the count closes and nothing fell off either end
! of the render.
!
! That is STRAP's semicolon: every card on this page is a
! double-statement card punched "SIC,STIC ; BD,SIPT+n.".  The idiom
! is already established in this corpus (printed pp.120, 126, 127,
! 129, 136, 140, 141, 142, 143 -- p.129 writes the one-card form in
! its deck and records that strap_asm splits on ';' at
! strap_asm.py:1010) and C22-6728-1 prints this very instance in
! prose as "(SIC,STIC; B,SIFAD)".  It is the largest run of it THIS
! TRANSCRIBER FOUND -- twenty-four consecutive semicolon cards and
! not one single-statement card, against the two-per-page the other
! decks record -- but NO ONE COUNTED RUN LENGTHS ACROSS THE LANDED
! DECKS, so treat "largest" as unmeasured.  printed p.175, rendered
! here for the join, carries four more of the same cards
! (KE000079..KE000082), so the run is really twenty-eight cards
! long and begins on the previous page.
!
! THE DECK WRITES THE ONE-CARD FORM, which is what the card is.  It
! was tried before it was written and it assembles to the printed
! words (see THE DECLARED CHECKS below).  Splitting the pairs onto
! forty-eight deck lines assembles identically; it was not done,
! because the card is the printed artifact.
!
! >>> AND IT SETTLES A QUESTION THE SIC RULE RAISES. <<<  strap_asm
! enforces "SIC must be immediately followed by a half-word branch"
! and docs/transcription_workflow.md records printed p.131 line 48's
! SIC and printed p.132 line 1's branch STRADDLING a page break.
! Those two decks' own notes give p.131's last card as ED000048 and
! p.132's first as ED000050 -- DIFFERENT CARDS -- so the pairing on
! THIS page is a punching style, not an assembler requirement.
!   (A LOOSE END IN THE REPO, CHEAP TO CHECK AND NOT CHECKED HERE:
!   those two notes leave ED000049 unaccounted for between the two
!   pages.  One render of either page's serial column would settle
!   whether a serial is missing or one of the two notes is off by
!   one.  It does not touch this page.)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ORIGIN IS ASSERTED, AND HERE IS EXACTLY WHAT BACKS IT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS NO SLC. <<<  Line 1 is a located instruction,
! so this deck writes a hard "SLC, (8)220650.0", and
! docs/transcription_workflow.md item 2 applies: a hard SLC CANNOT
! disagree with an arriving counter, it overrides it silently.  The
! forty-eight !CHECK rows below therefore ASSERT this origin rather
! than check it.  Two independent things back it, and they back
! different parts of it:
!
!   THE WORD, 220650, is the page header's own LOCATION field --
!   IBM's assembler stating line 1's location, read at 600 dpi.
!
!   THE BIT OFFSET, .00, is pinned by the page's own forty-eight
!   rows: the locations alternate .00/.40 across twenty-four words
!   with no drift.  An origin of 220650.40 would make line 2 print
!   220651.00, and it prints 220650.40.
!
! >>> THE BACKWARD JOIN TO printed p.175 IS NOW MEASURED OFF INK,
! THOUGH STILL NOT BY ANY AUTOMATED CHECK HERE. <<<  p.175 was
! rendered at 300 dpi (PDF p.182).  Its LAST printed row is line 56,
! "220647.40  215132.04 00  BD,SIPT+23." -- a HALF WORD, so the
! counter leaves p.175 at exactly 220650.00, which is this page's
! line 1 and this page's header LOCATION field.
!
! AND THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN IS UNBROKEN ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK:
! p.175's last two lines are KE000082 and this page's first two are
! KE000083.  Consecutive serials across a page boundary are a
! stronger join witness than either page's location column, because
! they are punched, not computed.
!
! When p.175's deck lands, the two should be compared by a single
! merged assembly; until then this is a hand comparison and it
! closes.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE CHECKS CAN AND CANNOT SEE, MEASURED NOT ASSERTED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> SIC PRINTS THE FULL 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS; BD PRINTS ONLY BIT 32.
! The two operations on this page are NOT equally informative, and
! the usual "an external back-derived from an instruction is known
! only to the half word" is HALF WRONG here.  Measured through
! strap_asm, varying ONLY the SYN and nothing else:
!
!   STIC SYN        SIC,STIC prints      SIPT SYN       BD,SIPT+24. prints
!   (8)215265.0     215265.00 80         (8)215103.0    215133.04 00
!   (8)215265.03    215265.03 80         (8)215103.03   215133.04 00
!   (8)215265.37    215265.37 80         (8)215103.37   215133.04 00
!   (8)215265.40    215265.40 80         (8)215103.40   215133.44 00
!
! So:
!
!   STIC IS PINNED TO THE BIT, twenty-four times over.  SIC's half
!   word is "bits 0-23 storage address (bit address), 24-27 literal
!   1000, 28-31 index" (Reference Manual printed p.41, cited at
!   strap_asm.enc_sic), so the printed 215265.00 is STIC's whole
!   address, offset included.  This page is therefore an
!   independent bit-exact reading of STIC, agreeing with its
!   DEFINING card -- printed p.116 line 16, "S T IC VF,0" at
!   215265.00, which decks/mcp_bootstrap_p116 !SYMs.  The ".0" that
!   twenty landed decks write for STIC is a convention on THEIR
!   pages; on this one it is a measurement.
!
!   SIPT IS PINNED ONLY TO THE HALF WORD, like every other branch
!   operand.  Bits 0-31 of SIPT are unobservable from this page;
!   only bit 32 shows, and it is 0.  SIPT's exact value comes from
!   its DEFINING card, printed p.112 line 7 ("S IPT BEW,$" at
!   215103.00, !SYM'd by decks/mcp_bootstrap_p112).
!
!   WHAT TWENTY-FOUR BD ROWS DO PIN is SIPT's WORD and the word
!   arithmetic: +24 through +47 land on 215133 through 215162
!   without a gap, twenty-four distinct printed words off one base,
!   so a one-word error in SIPT would fail all twenty-four at once.
!   The "+n" terms are DECIMAL and the printed words are octal --
!   +29 prints 215140, not 215134 -- which is checked twenty-four
!   times here.
!
! NO !SYM LINE IS DECLARED, because THIS PAGE DEFINES NO SYMBOL.
! The NAME column is empty on all forty-eight rows (the only ink in
! it is the word "NAME" in the heading).  SIPT and STIC are
! externals this deck SYNs from other pages' defining cards, so
! !SYM'ing them would check this deck against itself.  printed
! p.110's deck states the same rule for the same reason.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY IS WRONG AT BOTH ENDS AND HERE IS BY HOW MUCH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! strap/mcp_survey.py read this page as 34 rows over
! "220650.40".."206714.40".
!
!   THE ROW COUNT IS 48, not 34 -- fourteen short.  Counted off the
!   page's own line numbers 1* .. 48* AND independently off the
!   card-serial column (24 serials x 2 lines).  Two counts, one
!   answer.
!
!   THE FIRST LOCATION IS 220650.00, not 220650.40.  The survey lost
!   line 1, which is the loss docs/transcription_workflow.md warns
!   about by name.  The page header's LOCATION 220650 is the
!   independent witness.
!
!   THE LAST LOCATION IS 220677.40.  "206714.40" IS NOT A ROW ON
!   THIS PAGE and is not a row in MCP's body at all -- the body runs
!   above 215000 -- so it is a misread, most likely of 220677.40 or
!   of one of the 2151nn.04 operands in the BINARY OUTPUT column.
!   Nothing on this page carries a location below 220650.00 or above
!   220677.40.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page opens round glyphs constantly and says so itself: its
! own heading prints "LOCATICN" for LOCATION and "BINARY CUTPUT" for
! OUTPUT, and the header's time clock prints "00054041" as
! "C0054041".  In the body, "220650.00" prints as "220650.CC" on
! line 1 and "215265.00" prints as "215265.CC" on most SIC rows.
!
! EVERY ONE OF THOSE IS AN OCTAL FIELD, so every one of those C's is
! a 0 with certainty -- a C cannot occur in a location word, a
! location offset, or an address offset (rung 5 of the glyph ladder,
! docs/transcription_workflow.md 2b).  And rung 4, the strongest
! rung, fires twenty-four times on this page without being looked
! for: THE SAME STATEMENT IS PRINTED TWENTY-FOUR TIMES in the same
! character positions.  Line 7 prints "220653.00  215265.00 80" with
! closed zeros and line 1 prints the identical word with open ones.
! One page, one drum, one instruction, open and closed glyphs in the
! same column.  Nothing on this page rests on a single reading.
!
! The only two glyph pairs that could matter are in the mnemonics,
! and the assembler eliminates neither-nor: "SIC" and "BD" are both
! operations, so rung 3 is silent.  They are settled instead by the
! two landed decks printing the same mnemonics for the same words at
! the SIT addresses, by C22-6728-1's typeset "SIC,---; BD,---", and
! by the fact that no other reading of either string is an
! operation at all.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK: WHAT THIS PAGE RULES OUT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every one of the forty-eight line numbers carries an asterisk
! right after it ("1*", "48*").  That is the ordinary column, not a
! mark (printed pp.140, 141, 144, 145, 147 all record it).
!
! EXACTLY TWO lines carry a SECOND asterisk, after their LOCATION:
!
!   line  3   "220651.00 *"      SIC,STIC
!   line 26   "220664.40 *"      BD,SIPT+36.
!
! Both read off the 600-dpi render, not off any OCR, AND THE COUNT
! WAS MADE BY MACHINE RATHER THAN BY EYE, because "exactly two" is
! the load-bearing part.  The 400-dpi page was banded on the
! LOCATION column's own ink (50 bands: the time-clock line, the
! heading line, and the 48 rows -- which is a third independent
! confirmation of the row count) and the dark pixels in the
! asterisk column counted per band:
!
!   line  3   356 dark pixels   x 1782..1808   24 rows tall
!   line 26   381 dark pixels   x 1781..1806   24 rows tall
!   line 21    19 dark pixels   x 1827..1831    4 rows tall
!   all other rows:  0
!
! >>> LINE 21'S MARK IS NOT AN ASTERISK, AND IT IS RECORDED HERE SO
! THE NEXT READER NEED NOT RE-DERIVE IT. <<<  At 1200 dpi beside
! line 3's asterisk it is a single period-sized blob: one twentieth
! the ink, four pixels tall instead of twenty-four, sitting ON THE
! BASELINE where the asterisk is RAISED, and forty-five pixels
! (400 dpi) to the RIGHT of the column the two real asterisks share.
! It is in no field -- clear paper on all four sides -- and line
! 21's own row ("220662.00  215265.00 80  SIC,STIC") is
! bit-identical to the other twenty-three SIC rows and checks out.
! Drum noise, of the kind printed p.113's transcriber recorded on
! its own line 15.  It is also what the full-page 300-dpi view
! shows as a faint dot after "220662.CC", and at 300 dpi it is easy
! to call a third asterisk.  It is not one.
!
! NO RULE IS
! PROPOSED (the brief forbids it from one page, and printed p.151
! killed printed p.147's rule the same afternoon).  What this page
! can do is ELIMINATE, because it is the most uniform page in the
! block -- forty-eight structurally identical rows, two marked:
!
!   * NOT the statement, the operation, or the operand: line 3 is
!     bit-identical to the other twenty-three SIC rows and line 26
!     is the same shape as the other twenty-three BD rows.
!   * NOT the half-word parity: one mark is on a .00 row and the
!     other on a .40 row.
!   * NOT the presence of a NAME: no row on this page has one.
!   * NOT anything to do with SLC: THIS PAGE PRINTS NO SLC AT ALL
!     and still carries two marks.  That is a second independent
!     hit on the SLC family of hypotheses after printed p.151's.
!   * NOT the card: lines 3 and 26 are on different cards
!     (KE000084, KE000095) and neither card's OTHER line is marked.
!
! What survives, and is only an observation: TWO PER PAGE is what
! printed pp.140, 141, 144 and 146 also found, on pages with
! completely different content.  printed p.147 found three.
!
! THE ONE READING THAT WAS TRIED AND DIED IS ALREADY RECORDED, by
! printed p.113's transcriber, using THIS page: "the mark follows
! the slot" fails because line 26 branches to 215147, which is
! p.113's line 1 and is NOT marked, while line 3 is a "SIC,STIC"
! that branches nowhere.  That reading is dead and is not retried
! here.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! NO ASSEMBLER LIMITATION WAS HIT ON THIS PAGE.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every statement went in exactly as printed -- the unspaced
! "SIC,STIC", the trailing period on "BD,SIPT+24.", and the
! semicolon one-card form -- and was accepted first try.  Nothing
! was substituted, normalised or stubbed.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHERE THE COUNTER LEAVES, AND WHAT printed p.177 MUST PRINT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 48 is "BD,SIPT+47." at 220677.40, a HALF WORD, so
!
!     >>> THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT 220700.00. <<<
!
! There is no reservation, no forward SLC and no hole anywhere on
! this page, so that is a hard prediction and not an estimate.  It
! is falsifiable three ways on printed p.177:
!
!   1. If p.177's line 1 is a located row, its heading-row LOCATION
!      field must read 220700.
!   2. If p.177 opens with a printed "SLC,$", that SLC's own printed
!      LOCATION must read 220700.00 -- an SLC emits nothing, so its
!      location IS the arriving counter.
!   3. p.177's first EMITTING row must be at 220700.00.
!
! Only a forward "SLC,(8)nnnn." would legitimately break this.
!
! TWO THINGS ALREADY AGREE WITH IT, both from outside this page:
!
!   * decks/mcp_bootstrap_p117.strap -- LANDED AND CHECKED -- carries
!     "KFRONT SYN, (8)220700.0" and the !CHECK
!     "215501.40 | 220700.32 42 | line 19 BXL,K FRONT".  A branch
!     pins bit 32, and 0o32 has the 0o40 bit CLEAR, so K FRONT's
!     half word is 220700.00 and not 220700.40.
!   * the volume OCR of PDF p.184 (printed p.177, a DIFFERENT page)
!     prints an asterisk banner around "IDENTIFIER", then a row at
!     220700.00 carrying an asterisk and no binary (an "SLC,$"),
!     then "K FRONT  KVI,3,32.0" at 220700.00.
!
! So the expected shape of p.177 is: three banner lines (hence a
! BLANK heading-row LOCATION field there, by the line-1 rule), an
! "SLC,$" printing 220700.00, and K FRONT at 220700.00.  If p.177's
! heading LOCATION field turns out to be NUMBERED, one of the two
! readings above is wrong and this prediction fails loudly.
!
! A WARNING FOR WHOEVER DIFFS p.177: ITS "SLC,$" ROW AND ITS FIRST
! EMITTING ROW WILL PRINT THE SAME LOCATION, 220700.00, because an
! SLC emits nothing.  That is the "one location, two listing lines"
! case docs/transcription_workflow.md 3b describes; a diff that
! keeps one line per location will report a bogus mismatch there.
!
! C22-6728-1 names the routine: "control is given to the identifier
! routine (KFRONT)" / "The identifier is entered at KFRONT (Figure
! 18)".  It is the service-op identifier, not an operation branch
! table, and whatever p.177 turns out to contain, the ADDRESS is the
! thing this page predicts.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! 1. THE TWO LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISKS.  Eliminations above; no
!    rule.  This page is the best dataset in the volume for the
!    question (48 identical rows, 2 marked) and it still does not
!    answer it.
! 2. SIPT'S BIT OFFSET.  Twenty-four rows and all of them blind
!    below bit 32.  Its ".0" here is a convention carried from
!    printed p.112's defining card.
! 3. THE BACKWARD JOIN TO printed p.175 IS READ BUT NOT TESTED.
!    p.175's last row is 220647.40 and its last serial is KE000082,
!    both read off a 300-dpi render, and both agree with this page.
!    But no check in this repository compares the two until p.175's
!    deck lands and the merge runs.  That is a gap in the TESTING,
!    not in the reading.
! 4. WHICH MCP FUNCTION THE F ROUTINE SERVES.  p.175's own English
!    establishes WHAT it does with this table ("IS WD AN MCP ST
!    FXUP", "INSERT FIXUP INTO INTERPT TABLE", "STORE ST MCP FXUP
!    IN TABLE") and I believe that settles the table's purpose.
!    What I could NOT find is the F routine in C22-6728-1 by name,
!    so WHO calls it and WHEN -- IPL, a $ pseudo-op, an error
!    recovery -- is unestablished.  I did not grep exhaustively.
! 4a. WHETHER SLOTS 0..19 OF THE COPY EXIST ANYWHERE.  The copy
!    begins at F WIT = slot 20; nothing I read says what restores
!    indicators 0..19, and "FWIT-20.($4)" would index BELOW the
!    table for those.  Either $4 never goes below 20 on this path
!    or there is a table I have not seen.  printed p.175 lines
!    20-25 (the BRZ/BZB/KFI/BAE "SIC-BD ERROR" tests) are where
!    that would be bounded, and I did not read them closely.
! 5. ED000049, the serial unaccounted for between printed pp.131 and
!    132 in those two decks' own notes.  Noticed here, not settled
!    here, and it bears on how SIC/branch pairs were punched.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Both are SYN'd exactly as the landed decks spell them.  Neither
! carries a data description: neither defining card prints one
! ("S IPT BEW,$" is a place, "S T IC VF,0" is a VF), and no
! statement on this page takes its length from a symbol.
! NOTE, LOUDLY: p.112's defining card for SIPT is "BEW,$", a PLACE.
